Chamfers: the end of a chamfer in the OnSame state whose second contact
runs to the face at the end beyond that face's edge (mitcad#240).

The case: the foot of a rib on a wall, under the rib's bottom, chamfered
wider than the bottom (the rib's sides, prolonged down, cut the chamfer
along its edge and at its ends). At the ends of the spine the edges are in
the OnSame state: the contact on the wall runs on past the vertex, the
contact on the rib's bottom leaves it across a slanted edge.
ChFi3d_Builder::PerformIntersectionAtEnd updates the contact that runs
on past the vertex to the face at the end, where it meets that face's
edge prolonged (IntersUpdateOnSame). For the first contact the face's
surface is taken unbounded; for the second one (the same corner with the
chamfer's faces in the other order) it was taken bounded by the face, so
the point beyond the edge's end, outside the face, was not found and the
corner failed ("IntersectionAtEnd: pb intersection Face - Fi"). For a
chamfer it is unbounded now too. Fillets keep the bounded face: on a
rounding of a corpus design the unbounded one gave a rounding that is not
the body stored in the file, where the failing corner let the import take
the next definition, which is.

